  [NEW · SERIOUS · FACT] The body text gives HR 0.510 as the three-year recurrence result, but 0.510 is the five-year figure; the three-year figure is HR 0.501.
     quote         : “By the three-year readout at ASCO 2024, since published in full, the interval no longer crossed it: HR 0.510, 95% CI 0.288–0.906”
     why           : The draft holds the JCO Oncology Advances three-year paper and cites it as a source. That paper reports HR 0.501 at three years, not 0.510. HR 0.510 is the five-year point estimate, reported in the five-year JCO paper and the ASCO 2026 data. The CI (0.288–0.906) is correct for the three-year readout, so the interval has been correctly assigned to the wrong point estimate. A reader checking the three-year paper against this sentence will find a discrepancy in the headline number.
     fix           : Change 0.510 to 0.501 in this sentence. The five-year figure (0.510) is already correctly stated elsewhere in the piece.

  [NEW · SERIOUS · CONTRADICTION] The subheading 'The distinction the coverage collapsed' accuses the coverage of collapsing a distinction that the body immediately below it says the coverage did not collapse.
     quote         : “The distinction the coverage collapsed.”
     why           : The very next paragraph states: 'Every outlet whose article we hold attributed those figures correctly to KEYNOTE-942.' A subheading is a claim, and this one directly contradicts the finding reported beneath it. A reader who reads only the subheading takes away the opposite of what the body establishes. This is a contradiction within the piece.
     fix           : Replace the subheading with something that reflects what the body actually argues — e.g. 'Why correct attribution is not enough' or 'What correct attribution still leaves open.'

  [NEW · SERIOUS · THIRD_PARTY] The sentence 'That is what the headlines did' accuses unnamed headlines of substituting Phase 2b figures for Phase 3 figures without attribution, but the draft has established that every outlet it holds attributed correctly.
     quote         : “That is what the headlines did.”
     why           : The draft names five outlets and says all of them attributed the figures correctly to KEYNOTE-942. It then says 'that is what the headlines did' — meaning the substitution without attribution. The draft holds no example of a headline that performed this substitution without attribution. The accusation is directed at unnamed third parties and is unsupported by any instance the draft can point to. This is the same class of error the draft's own correction log flags: an accusation against coverage that the outlet-by-outlet check does not sustain.
     fix           : Replace with a sentence that describes the structural problem without accusing unnamed outlets of doing something the draft cannot evidence — e.g. 'The structure of the coverage creates that effect even when attribution is correct.'

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The lede frames the coverage as having failed ('what was actually released, what was not') in a way the body substantially retracts, creating a misleading first impression.
     quote         : “Several outlets called it a landmark. This is what was actually released, what was not, and what the numbers underneath it will and will not support.”
     why           : The juxtaposition of 'Several outlets called it a landmark' with 'This is what was actually released' implies the landmark characterisation was unwarranted or that the coverage misled. The body then says most specialist outlets attributed figures correctly and several explicitly noted no Phase 3 numbers had been released. Calling a first positive Phase 3 mRNA cancer therapy result a landmark is not obviously wrong. The lede sets up a critique the body does not deliver.
     fix           : Separate the two sentences or reframe: acknowledge the result is genuinely significant before pivoting to what the numbers do and do not show, so the lede matches the body's more nuanced conclusion.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The comparison table note says the Phase 3 is 'blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried,' but the draft's own next paragraph says the investigator-assessed endpoint means the bias is only substantially reduced, not eliminated.
     quote         : “It is the more trustworthy trial by a wide margin — seven times larger, and blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried.”
     why           : The phrase 'blinded against the assessment bias' implies the bias is fully addressed. The paragraph immediately following says the Phase 3 defines its primary endpoint as recurrence 'as assessed by the investigator' and that this 'leaves clinical judgement in the endpoint.' The draft's own correction log records that an earlier version said 'exactly' this bias and was softened — but 'blinded against' still overstates relative to what the next paragraph says. The two sentences are in tension.
     fix           : Change to 'substantially blinded against the assessment bias the earlier one carried' to match the qualification the very next paragraph provides.

  [NEW · MINOR · CALIBRATION] The draft says the April 2023 press release is 'the source of the one-sided p = 0.0266' and implies this value may appear nowhere else, but the ASCO 2023 abstract (LBA9503) also reports the same one-sided p = 0.0266.
     quote         : “We have not opened the conference abstract of the same analysis, so this page does not say the one-sided value appears nowhere but here.”
     why           : The draft is careful to hedge ('this page does not say the one-sided value appears nowhere but here'), which is honest. But the source note still singles out the April 2023 press release as the source in a way that implies the value is unusual or company-only. The ASCO 2023 abstract (LBA9503), which is publicly available, also reports the one-sided p = 0.0266 alongside the same HR and CI. The hedge is sufficient but the framing could mislead a reader into thinking the one-sided value was suppressed in academic venues.
     fix           : Add a parenthetical noting that the ASCO 2023 abstract also reports the one-sided value, or remove the implication that the press release is the sole source.

